---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Topbar from '../components/Topbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { SITE, BENCHMARK_FIELDS } from '../config';

const description =
  'What the CLI sends (nothing), what the optional --benchmark flag sends (twelve integers), ' +
  'and what this website records.';
---

<Base title="Privacy — verification-tax" description={description} path="/privacy">
  <Topbar />

  <main id="main" class="page">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="stamp">LAST UPDATED 6 SEPTEMBER 2026</p>

    <p>
      This page is short because there is not much to describe. If anything here turns out to be
      untrue, that is a bug and <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues`}>the issue tracker</a> is the place for
      it.
    </p>

    <h2>The command-line tool</h2>
    <p>
      <code>npx verification-tax</code> makes two kinds of network request: npm serves it to you, and
      it reads the GitHub API using the token already in your environment. <b
        >It sends nothing to us.</b
      > No account, no telemetry, no crash reporting, no phone-home version check.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your pull request metadata is read into memory, aggregated, and written to files next to you
      &mdash; <code>./tax.html</code>, <code>./tax.config.json</code> and the board slide. Nothing is
      uploaded. It never reads the contents of your code.
    </p>
    <p>
      You do not have to take our word for any of that. The source is MIT and the network calls are
      the easiest thing in it to audit.
    </p>

    <h2>The optional --benchmark flag</h2>
    <p>
      This is off unless you type it. When you do pass <code>--benchmark</code>, the tool sends
      twelve integers describing the shape of your engineering org, and nothing else:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {BENCHMARK_FIELDS.map(([name]) => <li><code>{name}</code></li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      There is no code, no file name, no repository name, no branch name, no author name, no company
      name and no email address in that payload. <a href="/benchmark">The full field list, with what
      each one means</a>, is on its own page.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you additionally pass <code>--contact</code>, an email address you type is attached, so the
      aggregate report can be sent to you when it publishes. That is the only circumstance in which
      the tool transmits anything that identifies you.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      The site is static files. There are no cookies, no local storage, no fingerprinting, no
      advertising or tracking pixels, and consequently no consent banner to dismiss.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <b>Analytics.</b> Plausible, which counts page views and referrers in aggregate without
        cookies and without storing anything that identifies a person. One custom event fires when
        you click a Copy button, so we can tell whether people actually take the command. That is
        the entire list.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Email signups.</b> If you give us an address for the aggregate report, it is stored by
        Buttondown, who send the mail. One email, when the report is out. Every message carries a
        one-click unsubscribe.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Hosting.</b> Cloudflare Pages serves the files and, like any web server, sees the IP
        addresses that request them.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        No data collected here is sold, rented, shared with advertisers, or used to build a profile
        of you. There is nothing to sell it to.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Getting your data removed</h2>
    <p>
      For the mailing list, use the unsubscribe link in any email, or <a
        href={`${SITE.repo}/issues`}>open an issue</a
      >. Benchmark submissions carry no identifier, so there is nothing in them to find and delete
      unless you passed <code>--contact</code>; if you did, the same routes apply.
    </p>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>
